# The Most Intuitive Way to Design Grids

`grid-template-areas` lets you literally draw your layout using strings. Each string is a row; each word is a named area.

```css
.container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-areas:
    "header header header"
    "sidebar main main"
    "footer footer footer";
}
```
This defines a 3×3 grid (3 rows, 3 columns). The words `header`, `sidebar`, `main`, `footer` are names that you can assign to grid items.

# Use `grid-area` on Items (Without Line Numbers)

Each grid item references the area name via `grid-area`:
```css
.site-header { grid-area: header; }
.sidebar   { grid-area: sidebar; }
.main-content { grid-area: main; }
.site-footer { grid-area: footer; }
```
No need to memorise line numbers! The item fills the entire rectangular area defined in the template.

slides.codeSnippet:
.container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-areas:
    "head head head"
    "nav  main aside"
    "foot foot foot";
  grid-template-columns: 200px 1fr 200px;
  grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto;
  min-height: 100vh;
}
.head { grid-area: head; }
.nav  { grid-area: nav; }
.main { grid-area: main; }
.aside { grid-area: aside; }
.foot { grid-area: foot; }

slides.content:
# Change the Layout at Breakpoints

You can redefine `grid-template-areas` inside media queries:
```css
@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .container {
    grid-template-areas:
      "head"
      "nav"
      "main"
      "aside"
      "foot";
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
}
```
This stacks everything vertically without touching the HTML!

# Use Both Together

You can define `grid-template-areas` and still use `grid-template-columns` and `grid-template-rows` to size the tracks. Areas give placement; line‑based properties size the tracks.

```css
.container {
  grid-template-areas: "a b";
  grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr;
  grid-template-rows: 100px;
}
```
This hybrid approach is common.
